# Draftly â€” Project Context

This is the shared handoff file for everyone working on Draftly: Claude Code,
Codex, and (by copy-paste) ChatGPT. Read **Current State** first. When you
finish a chunk of work, update it with the `update-context` skill
(`.agents/skills/update-context/SKILL.md`).

- **Current State** is a living snapshot â€” overwrite it as things change.
- **Log** is append-only, newest first â€” never rewrite old entries.

---

## Current State

Last updated: 2026-07-19 | by: Claude Code | repo @ 99426f0

### What Draftly is

A **lawyer-in-the-loop legal workflow platform for Sri Lankan legal practice**.
It has two main workflow tracks: **conveyancing** and **litigation**.
Conveyancing is the first prototype workflow because it is document-heavy,
rule-driven, and easier to evaluate; litigation is the second workflow track,
using an IRAC-style structure for later drafting and review. It is a DSE (ML +
Software Engineering) product, not a
research-only project â€” full software lifecycle, demoable at a symposium.

The core framing to preserve: **Draftly is not a form-filling PDF generator and
not a legal search engine.** It turns an unstructured legal/property document
bundle into a _verified, structured title record_, checks that record, and
generates multiple lawyer-reviewable drafts from it.

Core pipeline:

```text
Documents â†’ Extraction â†’ Linking â†’ Title graph â†’ Checks â†’ Lawyer verification â†’ Drafts
```

The differentiator vs "template filling": the template is only the final output
layer. The real work is before generation â€” reading prior deeds, extracting
facts, linking them across documents, reconstructing the chain of title,
resolving name variants, and flagging missing/inconsistent information. This is
also what makes it measurable with ML metrics.

### Academic context

- University of Moratuwa, Dept. of Computer Science & Engineering.
- Module `In23-S5-CS3501`; Project Idea submitted ~8 July 2026.
- Group 06: Dhanapala D.H.N., Dilshan A.D.L., De Silva B.K.P.
- Proposed Supervisor: Dr. Nisansa de Silva. Teaching Assistant: Ovindu A.
- Title: _Draftly: A Lawyer-in-the-Loop Legal Workflow Platform for Sri Lankan
  Legal Practice._
- Positioning: aligns with the broader legal-NLP direction of Dr. Nisansa's
  **SigmaLaw** work, but focused on practical drafting/workflow automation rather
  than legal search, sentiment, or case analytics. Existing SL platforms
  (LawLanka, AI PAZZ, Paralegal.lk, LawCeylon) mainly do research/legislation
  retrieval; no known end-to-end system reconstructs chain-of-title from a
  client's own deed bundle.

### Sri Lanka Law College past-paper collection

- `past-papers/` contains 44 validated PDFs (418,512,430 bytes): 19 files from
  the official Sri Lanka Law College downloads page and 25 public GovDoc archive
  files.
- The collection is organized into `entrance/`, `entrance-archive/`,
  `preliminary/`, `intermediate/`, and `final/`. It includes official year-level
  bundles through 2023, a 2024 general entrance set, model papers, and GovDoc
  language variants from 2015-2023 where publicly listed.
- `past-papers/manifest.csv` records the exact source URL, local path, year,
  level, medium, byte size, page count, download status, and SHA-256 for every
  PDF. All 44 entries passed PDF-header, `pdfinfo`, file-size, and checksum
  validation.
- The official 2020 Final Year link is only Civil Procedure and Pleadings I in
  all three media, not a complete Final Year bundle. Some later official bundles
  are scanned, so subject-level extraction (including LW 307 Conveyancing)
  requires OCR rather than filename or text-layer matching.

### First use case & outputs

Concrete first use case: **conveyancing title documentation.** Deliverable
outputs the system generates for lawyer review:

- Title report
- Pedigree / chain-of-title graph
- Abstract of Title Deed (AT form)
- Certificate of ownership
- Assessor / municipal ownership-transfer letter
- Deed schedule (à¶‹à¶´à¶½à·šà¶›à¶±à¶º)
- Missing-document checklist
- Red-flag / inconsistency report (DOCX/PDF export)

Architecture can support other workflows later (plaints, affidavits, petitions,
statements of objections, legal notices) but those are out of scope for v1.

### Inputs & extraction (schema, not data)

Input = a **property matter bundle**: current + prior deeds, gift deeds, survey
/ condominium plans, land-registry (path-iru) extracts, assessment-register
extracts, municipal receipts, identity docs (entered manually / anonymized),
existing title reports/pedigrees if any.

Fields extracted per source type (deeds: deed no/type/date, grantor/grantee,
consideration, notary, attestation, plan no, lot no, extent, boundaries,
registration refs; survey plans: plan no, surveyor, land name, lots, extent,
boundaries; municipal/assessment: assessment no, owner, annual value, receipt;
registry: volume/folio, owners, encumbrances, prior registrations).

Example red flags: prior deed referenced but not uploaded; owner name differs
across docs; extent/plan/lot mismatch; missing registration ref; undischarged
mortgage; a party transferring land they never acquired in the chain.

### Data Science components

OCR (Sinhala/English, historical + code-mixed), document classification, NER,
relation extraction, layout-aware extraction, table extraction, entity
resolution across name variants, cross-document linking, chain-of-title
reconstruction, defect/inconsistency detection, grounded generation with
clause-level provenance. Evaluated against lawyer-verified ground truth.

Metrics: classification accuracy, OCR accuracy, NER F1, key-field extraction
accuracy, relation-extraction accuracy, chain-of-title reconstruction accuracy,
defect-detection precision/recall, lawyer-rated draft correctness, provenance
correctness, time saved vs manual.

